  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 440 PM EDT TUE JUL 21 2026\n\nAt the onset of the long term period, upper level troughing will \nsit over the northeast CONUS, with an additional area of troughing\nassociated with a closed upper level low located off the Pacific \nNorthwest coast. Ridging will prevail between these features and \nwill be centered over the Southern Plains at the start of the \nextended period, with anomalously high 500 mb heights across much \nof the central and western CONUS. Locally, eastern Kentucky will \nfind itself in a region of northwesterly flow aloft for several \ndays, with the forecast area sandwiched between ridging and \ntroughing. At the surface, an area of high pressure near the Great\nLakes will keep winds northeasterly on Thursday, ushering in \ncooler and drier air. Highs will struggle to rise above the low \n80s, and with dewpoints falling into the upper 50s and low 60s, \nThursdays weather will be downright pleasant. Overnight, lows will\nplummet into the upper 50s and low 60s, with typical fog \nformation in our mainstem river valleys. \n\nPoPs will return by Friday afternoon as a shortwave embedded \nwithin northwesterly flow aloft rotates into our area. Ahead of \nthis shortwave, the eastward translation of the aforementioned \nsurface high will allow winds to gradually become more northerly \nto northwesterly over the course of the day. This will allow for \nmodest moisture advection, and given shortwave forcing, isolated \nto scattered storms will be possible near the VA line on Friday \nafternoon. With the potential for additional embedded impulses, \nrain chances will continue through Saturday, although the timing \nand details of this activity will need to be refined in later \nforecast periods. By Sunday, the northeast trough will deepen \nalong the Atlantic Coast, with flow aloft becoming increasingly \nnortherly over the forecast area. Model spread increases \nsignificantly at this point, with rain chances dependent on \nembedded impulses that are difficult to resolve this far out. For \nnow, slight to chance PoPs are forecast through the end of the \nperiod, although uncertainty remains high at this time.",
